Hey, we all start somewhere.

What I do is to have a copy of "command.com" in my
NetHack bones file directory, and a copy of the
hearse executable there too. That way instead of
just clicking on the hearse icon and having hearse
open its own MS-DOS window, do its thing, and close
the MS-DOS window faster than I can read whether
things went right, I can click the command.com icon,
have it open an MS-DOS window that _persists until I
dismiss it_, type "hearse" into that window to
execute hearse, read the results to make sure they
make sense, and then dismiss the MS-DOS window by
typing "exit" into it.

It's worth mentioning, since you're new to this,
that hearse has three modes of operation, which
you can select among by editing the text file
hearse.ini to make the one you like the default.

In one mode, "keep", hearse leaves the just dropped
bones file in your directory, copies it to the
hearse archive, and trades you back a bones file for
some random level for which you don't already have a
bones file. This way your set of bones files grows,
at the expense that some of them are bones files you
dropped yourself. I use this mode, but then, I like
hitting _lots_ of bones files in games, and as a
partial result, don't survive very long.

In a second mode "replace", hearse copies the bones
file you just dropped to the hearse archive, then
replaces the bones file you just dropped with a
different bones file for the very same level, if
possible. That way the bones files you have are new
to you, but appropriate to levels on which your PC
is currently dying, making them bones files you'll
probably encounter again soon. This is probably the
best choice for normal use.

In the third mode "lose", hearse copies the bones
file you just dropped to the hearse archive, removes
the bones file you just dropped, and returns a bones
file for some random bones level you don't already
have, instead. I _think_ that the bones file
returned tries to be "near" the level at which you
dropped a bones file, but I'm not sure of that.
The advantage of this mode is that so long as you
don't "peek" you won't know where to anticipate a
bones file might happen when playing next time.

